<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagera Region</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kagera Region is an administrative region of north-western Tanzania, bordering Uganda, Rwanda and Burundi, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> to the east and its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukoba">Bukoba</a>. Named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Kagera River</a>, it is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haya-people">Haya people</a>, whose small kingdoms such as Kyamutwara, Kiziba and Kianja survived under German and British rule until chieftaincies were abolished in 1963. Uganda's invasion of the region in 1978 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-tanzania-war">Uganda–Tanzania War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagera-region.jpg" alt="Kagera Region" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Macabe5387 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mater_Misericordiae_Church_-_Bukoba.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
